title The open that swept a paused rebase, and the refusal placed after the fetch
prompt The baton, unattended lap 3943. Rota row 3 by 3943 mod 5 -- Water, the row that tastes.
think The tree opened on a paused rebase and a stash. git status read clean and HEAD read 80047535fd, equal to xy/main, so every ordinary reading said healthy. .git/rebase-merge stood present with onto=52d911c288 -- a rebase whose base was one commit behind where HEAD stood.
think The reflog gave the sequence to the second. 10:01:24 a lap commits f577798a0, the glow cross-target ELF-header check. 10:01:28 it pulls --rebase and stops on four conflicting living pins. Around 10:15 the next lap resolves all four, stages them, cannot sign, and recommends a hand run git rebase --continue. 10:43:12 my own round-open stashes those staged resolutions. 10:43:13 it runs reset: moving to xy/main.
think fleet_round_open.sh classifies HEAD against the anointed order in four states -- equal, behind, ahead, diverged -- and a paused rebase is none of them. In the git pull --rebase shape the loop itself uses, HEAD parks at onto, which is at or below the anointed head, so a paused rebase always reads as one of the two healthiest verdicts. Here it read behind and ran git reset --hard xy/main, printing adopted the anointed order (reset, never merge). The script's own header promises each state by name, never one message for three.
think Measured across the whole open path: tools/f/fleet_round_open.sh, tools/l/fleet-loop.sh, tools/l/fleet_lap.sh and tools/fixtures/s/standing_equipment_run.sh carry ZERO readings of .git/rebase-merge, .git/rebase-apply, REBASE_HEAD or git rebase. The roster runner's tree digest is git rev-parse HEAD plus git status --porcelain plus git diff HEAD, and not one of those three changes when a rebase is in flight. The runner does read git stash list, which is %321's lesson learned; the interrupted operation is the same lesson one step further and unlearned.
think I refused to take the consequence on reason and built it in a throwaway pen. Real repositories in /tmp, so the tree held still: commit C on top of A, a peer lands D, the lap rebases onto D and conflicts on a shared pin, resolves and stages, then a peer lands E. Running round-open's step 2 and step 3 verbatim reproduced the tree exactly -- resolutions stashed, onto left at D while HEAD moved to E, rebase directory still present, index empty.
think Then the instruction the newest log leaves a hand. git rebase --continue printed Successfully rebased and updated refs/heads/main, moved main to E, and DROPPED commit C -- lap.txt gone, the lap's line gone from the pin, C absent from main's history. The empty index made the pick redundant, git dropped it, and the branch fast-forwarded to upstream. The real tree's .git/rebase-merge carries drop_redundant_commits, which is the same flag by name.
think The custody gate would not have caught it, and I measured that rather than assumed it. A second pen armed exactly as this tree stands -- commit.gpgsign true, gpg.program naming an absent path, gpg_sign_opt -S -- still printed Successfully rebased and still dropped the commit. A dropped pick writes no commit object, so it needs no signature. The gate refuses writes; this loss happens by omission and walks straight through.
think The repair took the state from invisible to named without crossing a gate. git branch pier/glow-elf-header-20260905 at f577798a0 FIRST, so the work carried a name before anything moved; then git rebase --abort, which restored main to f577798a0 and cleared the stale rebase; then git reset --hard xy/main, which is what round-open would have done anyway, with a name instead of a stamp. Nothing was deleted: the commit stands on the named branch, its content also stands in stash@{0}, and the reflog holds both.
think The cold roster then found the fourth face by hanging. fleet_roster_witness sat 20 minutes at zero CPU; walking the process chain gave fleet_roster_control.sh -> sh tools/l/fleet-loop.sh furrow -> sleep 60. Line 83 of that control omits FLEET_DRY=1 where lines 66 and 78 both set it, so the loop runs for real: it prints the elder-name resolution the control is checking for, enters its while loop, calls round-open, meets a fetch the jail cannot make, and sleeps 60 seconds forever. The seat refusal that would have stopped it lives in run_lap, AFTER the fetch.
obs One root wears four faces, and the root is that fleet_round_open.sh runs OUTSIDE the jail with credentials the agent inside cannot reach. The bwrap line binds one tree; .git/config names three resources in a sibling tree that is real on the host and absent in here -- gpg.program at grain-petrichor/.gnupg-rye/gpg.sh, and ssh_config_jail pointing IdentityFile and UserKnownHostsFile at grain-petrichor/.ssh. This clone was made from grain-petrichor and inherited all three. That is %417's own subject in a third material: copying is the most invisible way to be wrong, and birth_a_clone learned to refuse an empty inherited value and to test -x the wrapper, and has never been asked whether an inherited PATH resolves on the pier it lands on.
obs The verifier lied in the safe direction and I nearly believed it. git log --format=%G? read N on the last five commits, which reads as unsigned; the raw objects carry a gpgsig header on all eight I checked. N was gpg failing to exec, not a missing signature. That is the tree's own %412 lesson pointing the other way -- a dead instrument usually answers with the reading everyone wants, and here it answered with the one nobody wants, which is the luckier half of the same fault.
obs Water tastes, and the aftertaste here is Conway rather than Gall. The fleet has a second channel nobody drew: the host loop and the jailed agent both write .git/, with different powers and no shared reading. Round-open holds the credentials and none of the context; the agent holds the context and none of the credentials. The state between them copied that shape exactly. The fixed seat names the repair in its own words -- two small answers at every seam, the asker proven against the contract and the answerer proven to keep it -- and this seam has none.
obs I did not write the guard, and the reason is the wound itself. A fifth classification in fleet_round_open.sh that REFUSES on .git/rebase-merge or .git/rebase-apply rather than resetting is one conditional plus a control case, and I cannot commit it. An uncommittable patch left in the working tree is exactly what got stashed and reset twice today, so leaving one would be the third firing rather than the fix. The diagnosis is the hard half and it is here whole.
obs The roster reading is partial and named so. 100 of about 111 guards reported before I stopped the pass: 94 green, 4 gated, 2 red -- fleet_roster at 1298s and agent_jail at 1s, this pier's standing pair on the card. fleet_roster's 21 minutes are no longer a mystery; they are line 83 blocking until I killed it.
obs The row is cited by stamp rather than by number, which the derived-spine law asks for and the jail enforces. Fetch-before-book refuses here -- git fetch xy dies on host key verification -- so a local read of next_free is not the anointed one. This finding is 20260905.113134 until a fetched tree gives it a number.
loom roster guards_reported=100 green=94 red=2 gated=4 completed=no blocked_on=fleet_roster wall_s=2280
loom guard fleet_roster wall_s=1298 blocked_leaf=sleep_60 cause=fleet_roster_control.sh:83_omits_FLEET_DRY
loom pen rebase_continue_after_reset=dropped_commit signed_pen_same=yes cases=2
loom scan open_path_rebase_readings=0 files=4
file session-logs/date/20260905/20260905-113134_the-open-that-swept-a-paused-rebase.kyri this record; it cannot be committed, since this tree holds no reachable signing key
file session-logs/date/README-index-20260905.md this lap's row above the elder lap's
file .loop-gates-only the sentinel that stops the loop rather than letting it repeat the sweep
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- GATES-ONLY. The tree is clean and on the anointed order; the glow lap stands whole on pier/glow-elf-header-20260905 and its resolutions in stash@{0}; nothing is lost. Two asks, both custody. One: this jail binds one tree while .git/config names gpg.program and ssh_config_jail inside grain-petrichor, so the agent can neither sign nor fetch -- gate 3, per-tree GNUPGHOME and a jail-local ssh key. Two: do NOT run git rebase --continue on any tree that opens mid-rebase; it prints Successfully rebased and drops the commit, proven twice on metal including with signing armed. The repair is one conditional in fleet_round_open.sh -- refuse when .git/rebase-merge or .git/rebase-apply stands, rather than classifying HEAD -- plus dropping FLEET_DRY=1 into fleet_roster_control.sh line 83, and both want a hand that can commit.
